[[[ DELETE ]]]
The chain_copy concept has identified the delete problem and a possible
sollution for how to deal with it.

The problem is some modules work on a list of other modules. The list
stores raw pointers to the modules. If a module is deleted - freed from
memory, the list will contain a dangling pointer to invalid data without
any way for it to discern that.

Sollution 1) - synthmod flag (+ probably data object flag) earmarking
a module for deletion. module flagged for deletion instead of being
deleted, then moved from global user space list to private internal
deletion list. modules which contain lists of other modules then check
their data for modules flagged for deletion.

Sollution 2) - currently the pointer to a module stored in a list module
is obtained during definition (and the listed modules must already
exist). this would be changed so the pointers to listed modules would
be obtained during the validation phase, and return an error if not
found. this would require conversion of the list modules from using
dobjmod to modnamedobj (ie from pointer to a module to name of a
module).

Sollution 2 disregarding the extra work, is more appealing, but there
are further questions regarding grouping. if a module is grouped after
it's added to a list module, nothing currently needs to be done, however
if it becomes the name initially stored until validation, then the
stored name will no longer match and it becomes a potential match for
the way in which input/output connections work - grouping updates the
connections which name the previously ungrouped module.
HOWEVER, that requires a new global list storing all modnamedobj used in
all the list modules, and a scan of the list for module names to update.
So more extra work.


