I've returned to programming in a small way; it won't be as intensive as
in the past. This is in part thanks to social media! I've been making
short clips and started adding some of the sounds created long ago using
wcnt, to the clips. When I wanted to create new sounds, building wcnt
brought up lots of warnings from the compiler. Fixing them gave me a
little taster for coding again.

While using wcnt attempting to build more complex layers of sound, I
soon discovered its no easy task. My time away from it for well over
a decade provided fresh insight on the problems for users wcnt has.

When I first abstracted the code for reading and processing .wc files,
into something the user could use for including content from other .wc
files, one reason for doing so was the idea of creating commonly used
chains of modules for use by other files.

Later on, I implemented another method to re-use of chains of modules,
the capability to group modules together and then duplicate the entire
chain while preserving the input/output connection relationship from the
old group to the new group. This was paired with an input_editor and
param_editor in order to create variation amongst layers.

At the time I was really happy at achieving these goals, which had
taken wcnt beyond what I had originally ever envisioned it being
capable of. The reality however is, wcnt is quite clunky; these
mechanisms do not work as smoothly from a user's perspective as I'd
like.

I've come up with three ideas to address this issue: entry points,
automatic grouping, nameless data objects, and handful of other
improvements along the way.

This file has been used by me to think about these improvements through
writing about them. In the past I don't believe I ever thought through
my ideas to this extent before diving into changing and adding code to
the existing codebase.

=================
1) "Entry points"
=================

Suppose you create a whole bunch of modules in one file to create a
voice part. In another file you create somes riffs and a sequencer
using that voice part and output the result to a wav.

Every module in the voice part requiring sequencer outputs would need
manually connecting using the input_editor. The more modules within the
voice part requiring connection to sequencer outputs from another
file, the more difficult it becomes to keep track of what needed
connecting and to ensure they everything was fully connected in the
chain.

It caused me to consider what if I could create an "entry point" for a
file through which the file would connect to all the sequencer outputs,
and all the modules that needed them would be connected to these "entry
points" instead.

I realized that wcnt already has the mechanism to do this in the form of
wcnt_trigger and wcnt_signal modules. In the voice part file, generally
all that is needed is three wcnt_trigger modules to represent each of
the sequencer note_* trigger outputs, and two wcnt_signal modules to
represent its velocity and frequency outputs. All the modules in the
voice part file can then connect to these modules instead of initially
being set 'off' because in that file the sequencer did not exist.
to. Then when included, it's just a matter of connecting the entry
point modules to the sequencer outputs.

This worked well for reducing the number of modules and inputs for a
complex multi-layered voice part down to just 5 as an "entry point".

However, a strange error arose - wcnt insisted the wcnt_trigger module
didn't have an out_trig output. Some detective work revealed the
wcnt_trigger module uses SM_EMPTY_RUN. It's run method is empty, because
while it does register out_trig as it's output, it doesn't actually have
a variable for it, whatever output was connected to its in_trig input is
returned instead (so no need for a run method to transfer the input's
value to an output).

So in my use case, its in_trig input wasn't set when its out_trig
output was requested - it was still a NULL pointer - producing the
confusing error (as NULL returned by synthmod::base::get_out is taken as
an indication the output requested does not exist in the module).

I considered the idea of creating an actual output and scrapping the
SM_EMPTY_RUN method to solve the problem. But after some experimentation
I simply disliked that idea as a solution from the view point of
introducing uneccessary work to be done for every sample processed.
word.

Instead, I added a new module flag SM_PASSTHROUGH to handle the new case
for modules which pass their input directly as an output without any
processing (note: not all SM_EMPTY_RUN modules will need the new
SM_PASSTHROUGH flag, ie the constant module uses the parameter value as
output).

Now when the connection failed and both these SM_PASSTHROUGH and
SM_EMPTY_RUN flags were detected, the connection is postponed by moving
it from its current position in the connection list, to the very end.
The next time the connection in question is reached, then the connection
which sets the input used by the SM_PASSTHROUGH module should have then
been processed allowing something to be returned when its output is
requested!

The simplest basic use case is tested and works. It keeps tracks of the
existing tail of the connection list and upon reaching it (and there are
still more connections remaining) any connections thereafter, must be
postponed connections.

I suspect there may be more complex corner/edge cases which could
require postponed modules to be further postponed, but this is yet to be
tested. However, I don't think it would be overly complex to handle, and
the potential for an inifinite loop to occur can be limited by a simple
counter of how many times the former-tail is reached (and updated with
the new tail).

