=====================
2) Automatic grouping
=====================

For the idea of creating complex compositions with wcnt by using
multiple files for different layers of sound, we currently must ensure
each file uses unique module names to avoid name clashes.

Its no use within a non-real-time text-based modular system for it to
generate randomn names for things! Names need to be identifiable by the
user ahead of time. If the system is generating names, those generated
names must follow an easily predictable format.

The currently implemented way to generate unique names is for the user
to provide initial names to modules, and then group them using the group
data object with a unique username. Unfortunately this method is a
little awkward. Only after the modules have been created can they be
added to a group. To do so, the user has to specify all the modules to
be added to the group in the group definition. If there are many to add
then likelihood of user error increases. Additionally, if at a later
date the user changes the chain of modules either by adding or deleting
some, then the group definition will need to be updated also - when
production of a desired sound is the focus these snags can be causes of
disruption to the creative flow.

In the case of using multiple files (by including files using a
synth_file_reader data object), using groups to create unique names also
runs into problems. The modules are defined and created before grouping
occurs, so name clashes could still occur before the group is created
and the modules added to it.

This is where the idea of automatic grouping comes in. Automatic
grouping takes some of the burden away from the user for the task of
grouping modules.

Three initial ideas arose:

  i automatic grouping specified at the start of a file to apply to
    everything created within the file.
 ii some sort of autogroup directive or data object which can be placed
    anywhere in the file to initialize autogrouping, and then again
    anywhere later in the file to end autogrouping.
iii optional ability for synth_file_reader to group contents of a file
    into a specified group.

Options i is discredited in favour of ii, and option iii could still be
useful.

