--
What problem does grouping solve? Which problems arise from not being
able to group already grouped modules?
--

Firstly, grouping allows a selection of modules to be brought together
as a group. The group can be duplicated and the relationships between
the connections of inputs and outputs are preserved in the duplicate.
It reduces the effort required to build layers of similar sounds.

-- By preventing the addition of grouped modules to a new (parent) group
-- it is not possible to have more than one instance of a multi-layered
-- voice created by the use of grouping.
-- Why?
-- Because the method to create a multi-layered voice might rely on
-- duplicating a group to create each layer, if groups of groups of
-- modules are disallowed, this won't be possible.


How does grouping work?
It basically changes the name of objects in a predefined manner, and
updates the relevant connections to reflect the name change.
Each module name is prefixed by the username of the group data object
and the period (.) (usernames can't otherwise have a period within
them).

What if there was a degroup data object? A data object which removes the
modules from a group - but preserves the group name and replaces the
period (.) with another character (underscore perhaps)?

Mostly the purpose of grouping a collection of modules is simply to
duplicate many modules at once. In this case, once duplicated, grouping
has served its purpose.

However, given a voice with 10 layers, who is going to want to specify
in a group all of the de-grouped modules which go into creating 10
layers of sound? Not me!

